---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies entirely on router0, not on router1's WAN uplink as the ticket
speculated. The WAN link (wan_r0_r1, router1.eth2 ↔ router0.eth2) is physically
up and passes traffic without loss — a direct router1→router0 ping succeeds with
0% loss. The problem is that router0's RIP daemon (ripd) has been disabled:
/etc/frr/daemons contains "ripd=no", the /etc/frr/frr.conf file has been
deleted, and vtysh confirms "ripd is not running." As a consequence, router0
has never learned (and cannot advertise) routes via RIP. Its kernel routing
table contains only three directly connected prefixes — 10.0.0.0/8, 192.168.1.0/24,
and 192.168.2.0/24 — with no entries for the Office LAN (192.168.3.0/24) or
the Principle Room LAN (192.168.4.0/24). When a CS or IT host replies to an
Office or Principle host, the reply packet arrives at router0, which issues
"Network is unreachable" for those destinations and drops it. The forward
direction (Office/Principle→CS/IT) passes through router1, which still holds
recently aged RIP routes to 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24, but the return
path fails at router0, producing a one-way black hole that manifests as complete
timeout for both directions. The root cause is that ripd was disabled on
router0 — its daemons file was set to ripd=no and its frr.conf was removed.
Corrective fix: restore /etc/frr/frr.conf on router0 with a "router rip" stanza
advertising networks 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.2.0/24, and 10.0.0.0/8, change
"ripd=no" to "ripd=yes" in /etc/frr/daemons, and restart FRR (service frr
restart). Router0 will then advertise its directly connected subnets and learn
the Office/Principle routes from router1, restoring full bidirectional connectivity.
=== END ===
```